# The Dual Trace: Resonant Motion, Resonant Affect, and Temporal Navigation in Aristotle's Psychology

## 1. Aisthesis and Phantasia: From Perceptual Encounter to Image-Making

Aristotle's psychology proceeds from the conviction that the ensouled being stands open to the world through perception, and that from this openness a secondary capacity arises—one that retains, transforms, and re-presents what has been given in sensory encounter. Aisthesis, as the foundational act of perceptual awareness, is never merely the passive reception of data; rather, it involves the capacity of the perceiver to be affected by what lies between itself and the object. As Aristotle observes in *De Anima*, perception "is due to an affection or change of what has the perceptive faculty, and it cannot be affected by the seen colour itself; it remains that it must be affected by what comes between" (Aristotle, *On The Soul (De Anima)[1]*, pp. 29–30). Thus the perceptual encounter already implicates a mediating structure, and it is precisely this mediation—this interval between object and organ—that opens the space within which phantasia subsequently operates. Phantasia, while rooted in and dependent upon aisthesis, is not identical with it; rather, it contrasts with perception insofar as it involves the capacity to generate images (phantasmata) even in the absence of the external object that initially provoked the sensory affection. Indeed, Bowin has noted that commentators implicate phantasia in special, common, and incidental perception alike (Bowin, *Aristotle on the Perception and Cognition of Time*, pp. 17–19), underscoring how thoroughly this image-making power pervades the soul's encounter with the world. We the internal distinctions within phantasia—its sensory and deliberative grades, its complex relationship to belief or doxa, and its voluntary or involuntary character—will be examined in detail in subsequent sections, specifically in our treatment of phantasia and doxa below. 

## 2. Kinesis and Dynamis: Motion as the Ontological Ground of Soul-Activity

If phantasia presupposes aisthesis, both in turn presuppose something still more fundamental: motion, or kinesis, which constitutes the ontological ground upon which all soul-activity unfolds. Aristotle's account of the soul is inseparable from his broader physics of motion, for the soul is not a static substance but a principle of activity whose being is realized in and through movement. In the *Metaphysics*, Aristotle clarifies that "being has two forms—it exists not only in fulfillment but also as matter" (Aristotle, *Metaphysics*, pp. 156–157)[2], and it is this dual modality of being—as actuality and potentiality—that underwrites the soul's capacity to move from perception to image, from image to memory, and from memory to deliberative action. Heidegger, in his lectures on Aristotelian philosophy, deepens this insight by arguing that the being itself "is determined in its being as circumscribed by the πέρας" (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, pp. 25–26)[3], suggesting that the soul's activity is always bounded and oriented, always a motion that moves toward or away from some definite limit. Accordingly, Gonzalez articulates the causal chain at work in Aristotle's psychology: "The causal chain is: 'thought' or 'sense perception' - phantasia - 'desire' - 'bodily affections' - motion" (Gonzalez, *The Meaning and Function of Phantasia in Aristotle's 'Rhetoric' III.1*, pp. 23–24)[4]. This chain reveals that phantasia occupies a mediating position between perception and desire, between the cognitive and the appetitive, and hence that the kinetic character of the soul is not merely locomotion but the very movement of psychic life through its successive phases. Motion, thus understood, is not incidental to the soul but constitutive of its manner of being in the world. 

## 3. Apoleipomenē Kinēsis: The Residual Motion and the Genesis of the Phantasma

The transition from aisthesis to phantasia is mediated by what Aristotle calls the residual motion—the apoleipomenē kinēsis—which persists in the sensory apparatus after the external object has ceased to act upon it. This residual motion is the genesis of the phantasma, the image that lingers and can be recalled even when the original perceptual encounter has ended. Aristotle's account in the *Physics* recognizes that not all motions experienced by the animal are self-initiated; indeed, "the cause of it is not derived from the animal itself: there are other natural motions in animals, which they do not experience through their own instrumentality, e.g. increase, decrease, and respiration" (Aristotle, *Physics*, p. 121)[5]. Similarly, the residual motion that gives rise to the phantasma is not a motion the perceiver deliberately sets in operation; it is a natural consequence of having been affected by the perceptible object, a kind of kinetic afterimage that the medium and the sense organ continue to sustain. This notion aligns with Aristotle's broader potentiality and actuality are intertwined, such that what has been actualized in perception leaves behind a trace in the form of potential re-actualization (Aristotle, *Metaphysics*, pp. 156–157). Gonzalez underscores the centrality of this kinetic residue to Aristotle's rhetoric, noting that phantasia functions not as a separate intellectual power but as the movement that carries perceptual content forward into imagination and desire (Gonzalez, *The Meaning and Function of Phantasia in Aristotle's 'Rhetoric' III.1*, pp. 23–24). Hence, the phantasma is not a copy or picture stored in the soul; it is a persisting motion, a dynamic trace whose kinetic nature ensures that it can be reactivated under appropriate conditions. 

## 4. Phantasia and Mnēmē: The Kinetic Trace as the Basis of Memory

The relationship between phantasia and memory (mnēmē) is one of the most consequential in Aristotle's psychology, for it is through the kinetic trace—the residual motion that constitutes the phantasma—that the ensouled being gains access to the past. Memory, for Aristotle, is not a storehouse of fixed representations; it is the re-engagement of a motion that was once initiated by perception and that continues to resonate in the soul's sensory and imaginative faculties. As Aristotle explains, "sometimes in remembering a fact one has no determinate time-notion of it, no such notion as that, e.g., he did something or other on the day before yesterday; while in other cases he has a determinate notion of the time" (Aristotle, *On Memory*, pp. 8–9)[6]. This passage reveals that memory involves not merely the re-presentation of an image but also a temporal indexing—a recognition that the phantasma pertains to a past event, even when the specific temporal coordinates remain indeterminate. Papachristou's work on the grades of phantasia illuminates this connection by observing that Aristotle distinguishes among different levels of phantasia operative in different kinds of animals: "Therefore some other animals have memory, and not only human beings and those beings that have opinion or judgment" (Papachristou, *Three Kinds or Grades of Phantasia in Aristotle's De Anima*, pp. 21–23)[7]. This remark indicates that the kinetic trace underlying memory is present even in beings that lack the higher deliberative phantasia associated with doxa and rational judgment—a distinction we shall examine in greater detail in our discussion of phantasia and doxa below. Specifically, the sensory grade of phantasia suffices for memory in non-rational animals, whereas deliberative phantasia enables human beings to combine memory with temporal judgment and evaluative reflection. Thus, the kinetic trace is the basis of memory precisely because it preserves not only the content of the original perception but also something of its affective and temporal character, thereby allowing the ensouled being to navigate the difference between present experience and past encounter. 

## 5. Resonant Motion and Resonant Affect: The Dual Trace Structure

We are now in a position to articulate what we call the dual trace structure—the intertwined persistence of resonant motion and resonant affect within the ensouled being's psychic life. The phantasma, as we have seen, is a kinetic residue, a motion that persists after the perceptual encounter has ceased; but this motion is never purely cognitive or representational. It carries with it an affective charge, a coloring of pleasure or pain, attraction or aversion, that is inseparable from the image itself. the form and the essence of a body of a certain kind" (Aristotle, *Metaphysics*, pp. 86–87) implies that all psychic activity, including phantasia, is embodied activity—activity that necessarily involves the body's affective dispositions. The dual trace, accordingly, consists of a kinetic-cognitive dimension (the image as residual motion) and a kinetic-affective dimension (the emotional resonance that accompanies the image). Gonzalez's articulation of the causal chain—from thought or sense perception through phantasia to desire and bodily affections—confirms that phantasia does not operate in isolation from the passions but rather mediates between cognitive content and bodily feeling (Gonzalez, *The Meaning and Function of Phantasia in Aristotle's 'Rhetoric' III.1*, pp. 23–24). Rickert, drawing on the phenomenological tradition, environments generate "an overarching ambient environment that cradles, without forcing, particular affective responses or that fosters an attunement" (Rickert, *Ambient Rhetoric*, pp. 172–174)[8]. The dual trace, then, is the mechanism by which the ensouled being retains not merely what it perceived but how that perception felt, and it is this dual retention that makes possible both memory and deliberation. 

## 6. Phantasia and Doxa: Judgment, Opinion, and the Evaluative Dimension

The relationship between phantasia and doxa—judgment or opinion—is essential for understanding the evaluative dimension of Aristotle's psychology. Phantasia, as a faculty distinct from both perception and intellect, occupies a mediating position that allows images to be accompanied by, yet not reducible to, acts of judgment. Aristotle is careful to distinguish phantasia from doxa: phantasia can occur in non-rational animals that lack the capacity for belief, whereas doxa requires logos and rational conviction. As Papachristou observes, memory belongs not only to human beings but also to animals that lack opinion or judgment (Papachristou, *Three Kinds or Grades of Phantasia in Aristotle's De Anima*, pp. 21–23), which confirms that the sensory grade of phantasia can operate independently of doxa. Nevertheless, in human beings the two faculties frequently cooperate; when phantasia rises to its deliberative grade, images become the material upon which doxa operates, enabling the agent to form opinions about what is good, useful, or threatening. Gonzalez's analysis of the causal chain clarifies that the relationship between phantasia and desire is mediated by the capacity for thought, so that when doxa is present, the entire chain from perception to action is inflected by evaluative judgment (Gonzalez, *The Meaning and Function of Phantasia in Aristotle's 'Rhetoric' III.1*, pp. 23–24). Furthermore, Aristotle's rhetorical theory depends upon the speaker's ability to place things "before the eyes" of the audience, invoking phantasia in a way that shapes doxa; as Hawhee notes, epideictic speeches "take up actions that are agreed upon, so that what remains is to clothe the actions with greatness (megethos) and beauty" (Hawhee, *Looking Into Aristotle's Eyes*, pp. 12–13)[9]. Thus, the voluntariness of phantasia in its deliberative mode is precisely what allows the rhetor to guide the audience's evaluative orientation, shaping judgment through the strategic deployment of images. The distinction between sensory phantasia (involuntary and shared with non-rational animals) and deliberative phantasia (voluntary and uniquely human) is therefore crucial for understanding how doxa and phantasia interact in the ensouled being's practical and rhetorical life. 

## 7. Stimmung and Attunement: The Affective Trace as Ambient Orientation

The concept of Stimmung—attunement or mood—provides a philosophical vocabulary for articulating the affective dimension of the dual trace. If the resonant motion is the kinetic-cognitive aspect of the phantasma, then Stimmung names the ambient affective orientation that accompanies and colors the ensouled being's engagement with the world. Heidegger's interpretation of the Greek pathe as Befindlichkeiten—as ways of finding oneself disposed in a situation—transforms our understanding of Aristotle's passions from discrete emotional episodes into pervasive atmospheric conditions. The index to *Heidegger and Rhetoric* registers "Attunement (Befindlichkeit)[13]" alongside aisthesis and the doctrine of affections (Multiple Authors, *Heidegger and Rhetoric*, pp. 199–200), thereby confirming that Heidegger's reading locates attunement at the intersection of rhetoric, perception, and affective life. Rickert extends this insight by arguing that environments do not merely contain objects for perception but actively generate ambient conditions that foster particular attunements, "without forcing, particular affective responses" (Rickert, *Ambient Rhetoric*, pp. 172–174)[10]. Gibson's ecological approach similarly challenges the assumption that perception is built from discrete sensory inputs; he critiques the view that "sensations are converted into perceptions" and that "every item of experience has to be carried forward into the present in order to make possible perception in the present," calling this framework "full of difficulties" and noting that "the error was to suppose in the first place that perception of the environment is based on a sequence of discrete images" (Gibson, *The Ecological Approach to Visual Perception*, pp. 245–246)[11]. Gibson's critique supports the notion that the affective trace is not a stored image but an ambient orientation, a way of being situated that shapes what the perceiver is prepared to encounter. Hence the dual trace structure—resonant motion and resonant affect—should be understood not as two separate items stored in the soul but as two inseparable dimensions of a single ambient attunement that pervades the ensouled being's temporal existence. 

## 8. The Dual Trace and Temporal Navigation: How the Ensouled Being Navigates Time

The culminating insight of the dual trace framework is that the ensouled being navigates time not through abstract cognition of clock-time but through the interplay of resonant motion and resonant affect. Memory, anticipation, and deliberation all depend upon the soul's capacity to re-engage kinetic traces—phantasmata that carry both cognitive content and affective coloring—and to orient itself within a temporal horizon. Heidegger's critique of the levelled-off "now" in *Being and Time* underscores the inadequacy of the ordinary conception of time as a mere sequence of present moments (Heidegger, *Being and Time*, p. 501). Indeed, Aristotle's own account of temporal navigation involves something more than locating events on a linear timeline; it requires the recognition that a phantasma belongs to the past, even when the specific time remains indeterminate (Aristotle, *On Memory*, pp. 8–9). Burke's dramatistic framework illuminates the complexity subsumed beneath any apparently simple motive, urging that "when we confront a simplicity we must forthwith ask ourselves what complexities are subsumed beneath it" (Burke, *A Grammar of Motives*, pp. 126–127)[12]. Similarly, the dual trace is not a simple mechanism; it is the complex interplay of motion and affect that enables the ensouled being to move between past and future, to recall not merely what happened but how it felt, and thus to orient its actions in the present. Rickert's notion of ambient attunement completes this picture by suggesting that the affective trace is not merely a residue of the past but an ongoing condition of openness to the world (Rickert, *Ambient Rhetoric*, pp. 172–174). Accordingly, temporal navigation is not a purely intellectual achievement; it is an embodied, affective, and kinetic process in which the dual trace—resonant motion and resonant affect—serves as the medium through which the ensouled being finds its way through time. ---

